{
  // Cloudflare Workers config for bracemark-extractor — the opt-in, anonymous link-
  // metadata fetcher on its OWN origin (extractor.bracemark.com), deliberately SEPARATE
  // from the sync broker on api.bracemark.com so that "api.bracemark.com never sees your
  // library in plaintext" stays code-provable: the extractor is the one component that
  // `fetch`es arbitrary, user-supplied URLs. NOT "only ever sees ciphertext" — that api
  // also serves auth and billing and holds a username, sessions and a purchases row in the
  // clear. See docs/link-extraction.md ("server extraction") and docs/architecture.md.
  //
  // Note the deliberately MINIMAL binding set vs. bracemark-api: NO D1, NO R2, NO
  // Durable Objects. The extractor is a PURE FUNCTION — it holds no key, persists
  // nothing, writes no blob — so it has no storage bindings to leak through. Its
  // only bindings are CORS config + native rate limiters. Workers is a good fit
  // precisely because its sandbox can't reach a private network or cloud IMDS
  // (169.254.169.254), which neutralizes the classic server-side-fetch SSRF.
  "$schema": "../../node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "bracemark-extractor",
  // Workers entry: src/worker.ts default-exports the Hono app; the runtime calls
  // fetch(request, env, ctx) on it.
  "main": "src/worker.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-06-01",
  // Observability with the INVOCATION LOG OFF — and it stays off although no
  // request line here carries a saved address any more. Cloudflare's automatic
  // per-request log records the request line, and the image proxy used to be
  // `GET /v1/image?url=…`, the user's URL in its query string; left at its default,
  // `invocation_logs` would have PERSISTED exactly the URLs docs/link-extraction.md
  // (_never log the URL_) promises stay transient. Both endpoints are POSTs now and
  // the address travels in the body, which the same log on bracemark-api was not
  // seen to store (docs/api-contracts.md) — but "the body is unlogged" is
  // Cloudflare's behaviour on Cloudflare's schedule, and "the log is off" is ours.
  // The privacy policy prints the second one
  // (apps/bracemark-site/src/app/privacy/page.tsx, _Technical and security data_,
  // and _link previews_: "writes none to its logs"), so turning this on falsifies
  // a published sentence whatever the request line holds.
  //
  // What survives: everything we log ON PURPOSE. `lib/errors.ts` and
  // `routes/extract.ts` are already written to this rule — the handler comment says
  // "never the URL", `SafeFetchError`'s message is its error code, and extractOne
  // logs `err.name` alone — so a 500 still reaches `wrangler tail` with its cause,
  // minus the address. KEEP THAT DISCIPLINE: a future `console.error(err)` where the
  // error embeds a URL re-opens this hole, and no test can see it.
  //
  // Rate limiting is UNAFFECTED — it runs on the native binding keyed by
  // `cf-connecting-ip` (middleware/rate-limit.ts), and never reads a log.
  //
  // NEVER add `"logpush": true` here. It reads like a sibling of the settings in
  // this block and is a different CHANNEL: `observability` governs Cloudflare's
  // own log (what `wrangler tail` and the dashboard show), while `logpush` ships
  // this Worker's TRACE EVENTS off Cloudflare to a destination you configure —
  // and a trace event carries the request line, so `invocation_logs: false` does
  // NOT cover it. The key would ship this Worker's requests — the stream the
  // paragraph above exists to keep unrecorded — into third-party storage, and it is
  // silent in the usual way: no lint error, no type error, nothing either runner
  // can observe. It needs a Workers Paid plan to take effect, so a repo that
  // upgrades its plan later is the case to watch.
  //
  // Not covered here: zone-level Logpush / HTTP request logging is a THIRD
  // channel — a Cloudflare setting on the extractor.bracemark.com zone rather than
  // anything in this file. It must stay off for the same reason, and nothing in
  // this repo can assert that it is. (Zone datasets are Enterprise-only, which is
  // what keeps it off by default rather than any choice made here.)
  //
  // `traces` is pinned OFF for the same reason, and it is the sharper half. A
  // trace records a span for every outbound `fetch`, and this Worker's outbound
  // fetch IS the saved address — so tracing could store what `invocation_logs:
  // false` exists to keep out, by a different door. Today `enabled: true` turns
  // on logs alone, but only because Cloudflare says so while tracing is in beta;
  // that default can move on their release with no change here, so the answer is
  // written down instead of inherited.
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
    "logs": {
      "invocation_logs": false,
    },
    "traces": {
      "enabled": false,
    },
  },
  // Pin a distinct local port so it can run alongside bracemark-api (8787) under
  // `wrangler dev` without colliding / silently drifting to a random port. The
  // V8 `inspector_port` (default 9229) is a SEPARATE socket not covered by
  // `port`, so pin a distinct one too — otherwise it collides with bracemark-api's
  // 9229 ("Address already in use (127.0.0.1:9229)").
  //
  // `ip` defaults to `localhost` — loopback only, so another device on the LAN
  // (a phone loading bracemark-web off this Mac) gets a connection refused here
  // while the Next dev server on 3000, which binds `0.0.0.0` by default, answers
  // fine. Matches bracemark-api's dev block; dev-only, never a deployed env. See
  // docs/env-files.md, _the local port map_.
  "dev": {
    "ip": "0.0.0.0",
    "port": 8788,
    "inspector_port": 9230,
  },
  // --- per-tier environments -------------------------------------------------
  // Bindings (`vars`, `ratelimits`) are NOT inherited from the top level — each
  // environment declares its own. Worker names auto-suffix to
  // bracemark-extractor-staging / bracemark-extractor-production. There are no
  // secrets here: v1 is ANONYMOUS (no session, no key), so there's nothing to
  // `wrangler secret put`.
  //
  // NO `account_id` HERE, deliberately — the same call as bracemark-api's
  // wrangler.jsonc, for the same reason: each tier IS a separate Cloudflare
  // account, but this file is public and an account id is inventory. The target
  // comes from CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID in the deploy environment:
  //   CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=<staging id> wrangler deploy --env staging
  // On a dev machine it comes from the gitignored `.env.<tier>.local` beside this
  // file, which wrangler loads only for that `--env` (docs/env-files.md,
  // _bracemark-api_ — the same pair of files lives here).
  // With nothing pinning the account in the config, the API token's scope is the
  // only thing left that stops a wrong-tier deploy — always _Specific account_.
  "env": {
    // Local-only — used by `wrangler dev --env development`, never deployed.
    "development": {
      "vars": {
        // Comma-separated exact origins (corsOrigins() splits on ','). The web app
        // (bracemark-web) is the browser caller that needs CORS; the extension and the
        // future mobile app are NOT browser-CORS-bound (extension background fetch
        // is exempt; native has no CORS), so don't add extension/native origins.
        "CORS_ORIGINS": "http://localhost:3000",
      },
      // Native rate-limit bindings, one per volume tier (middleware/rate-limit.ts).
      // period is 10 or 60 only; 'tight' (10/10s ≈ 1 req/s) is the strictest the
      // native binding allows. Both fetch endpoints fetch arbitrary URLs — an
      // anonymous open-fetch endpoint is an open proxy / DDoS reflector by default,
      // so the IP rate limit is load-bearing, not a nicety (the per-response byte
      // ceiling + timeout in the fetch path are the other half). /v1/extract runs on
      // 'tight' (one batched request per page); /v1/fetch-image runs on the higher-burst
      // 'image' tier (30/10s) because a page fans out to one image fetch per link —
      // but 'standard' (60/60s) on the same path still governs its sustained egress.
      // namespace_id is any unique positive integer.
      "ratelimits": [
        {
          "name": "API_RATE_LIMIT",
          "namespace_id": "2001",
          "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 },
        },
        {
          "name": "API_RATE_LIMIT_TIGHT",
          "namespace_id": "2002",
          "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 10 },
        },
        {
          "name": "API_RATE_LIMIT_IMAGE",
          "namespace_id": "2003",
          "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 10 },
        },
      ],
    },
    "staging": {
      "vars": {
        // Web origins ONLY (see development note). The web app is the CORS caller.
        // Staging lives on its OWN registrable domain (dryrunbase.com), not a
        // subdomain of bracemark.com — docs/hosting.md, _why staging is a
        // separate domain_.
        "CORS_ORIGINS": "https://dryrunbase.com,https://app.dryrunbase.com",
      },
      "ratelimits": [
        {
          "name": "API_RATE_LIMIT",
          "namespace_id": "2001",
          "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 },
        },
        {
          "name": "API_RATE_LIMIT_TIGHT",
          "namespace_id": "2002",
          "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 10 },
        },
        {
          "name": "API_RATE_LIMIT_IMAGE",
          "namespace_id": "2003",
          "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 10 },
        },
      ],
      "routes": [{ "pattern": "extractor.dryrunbase.com", "custom_domain": true }],
      //
      // IMPORTANT — never log the URL. No request line carries one any more (both
      // endpoints take the address in a POST body), so what is left that could
      // PERSIST a URL the design promises not to retain is whatever records what
      // this Worker DOES rather than what it was asked: tracing (a span per
      // outbound fetch — pinned off above) and a `console.*` call that prints a
      // request or an error whole. Logpush stays off for its own reason, also
      // above. See docs/link-extraction.md ("Never log the URL").
    },
    "production": {
      "vars": {
        "CORS_ORIGINS": "https://bracemark.com,https://app.bracemark.com",
      },
      "ratelimits": [
        {
          "name": "API_RATE_LIMIT",
          "namespace_id": "2001",
          "simple": { "limit": 60, "period": 60 },
        },
        {
          "name": "API_RATE_LIMIT_TIGHT",
          "namespace_id": "2002",
          "simple": { "limit": 10, "period": 10 },
        },
        {
          "name": "API_RATE_LIMIT_IMAGE",
          "namespace_id": "2003",
          "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 10 },
        },
      ],
      "routes": [{ "pattern": "extractor.bracemark.com", "custom_domain": true }],
      // See the staging note on never logging the URL.
    },
  },
}
